7
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:43,540
Well, welcome, everybody.

8
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:46,840
My name is Rachel, and this is my friend Jang-nyung.

9
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:51,620
And she is somewhere... it's cold

10
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:56,280
But she's going to be ambitious, and she's going to go to the gym

11
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:58,700
to finish up her conversation.

12
00:00:58,700 --> 00:00:59,820
And I think you might understand why after our conversation.

13
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:00,000
So, we're going to sit in a extensions chair for a while.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:11,000
So Nia, we were talking about looking back over this past year, looking forward and looking at making goals and taking care of ourselves.

15
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,000
So would you like to share what you came up with? Because I know you put a lot of thought into this.

16
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Sure. As a person living with dementia, I find that even though there are things that I can no longer do, it's important, more important to focus on what I can do.

17
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:47,000
And that doesn't mean that I can't have goals for myself. And so for the new year, I went ahead and came up with some new years, not really resolutions, but goals to where I can engage my mind, spirit and body.

18
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:58,000
And they're done by the week to make them. So they're short term goals, but they're things that can help people like me living with dementia to live well and live better lives.

19
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Thank you.

20
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
I like that idea of putting it by the week.

21
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Yes, by the week. For instance, when it comes to the mind, these are some of my things that I'm doing. I'm completing a one crossword puzzle a week.

22
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:26,000
So that is my goal, because sometimes, you know, we find that we may sit and watch a little bit too much TV.

23
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,000
But for those of us living with dementia,

24
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
we really need to engage our minds.

25
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:37,000
So that is one of my goals is as and also journaling my thoughts.

26
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Because although we may be in support groups, and we may have friends and family, it's important to also get other feelings out that we may not be able to tell anyone else.

27
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:57,000
And so journaling our thoughts and also in a positive way that is also leaving your thoughts for your loved ones.

28
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
While your mind is still fresh and good.

29
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
And the next one is embracing gratitude and possibly just saying thank you more every day.

30
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Whether it just be, you know, when I wake up in the morning, the first thing that I say is thank you, Lord, because he woke me up.

31
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
So embracing a spirit of gratitude.

32
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
And along those lines, also for every negative thing, think a positive.

33
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Proclaim something positive.

34
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:43,000
And that will also help our minds to think more, get those endorphins going and just think more, get the dopamine going and just engage those other cells in our minds.

35
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:51,000
And the last one for your mind that I'm doing is I'm revisiting a hobby, or I may even try a new one.

36
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
I used to be able to sketch, Rachel.

37
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
I used to be able to draw anything that I could see.

38
00:03:58,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Wow.

39
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,000
So I'm going to try and sketch again.

40
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,000
So those are a few things that I'm doing to engage my mind in the new year.

41
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:15,000
And my goal is to sketch something or engage in a new hobby every week.

42
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:24,000
And some things that you may be able to do is to check out the, if you have an adult community college, sometimes they have like cooking classes.

43
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Or learning instrument classes.

44
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,000
These are things that people living with dementia can do.

45
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,000
We can still do these things.

46
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,000
As Rachel, as you said, I'm going to the gym when I get out of here.

47
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
And one of my goals, we have to, if it's good for the heart, it's good for the mind.

48
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,000
So my goal is to exercise three to four times a week.

49
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:52,000
And to also go for walks.

50
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
And drinking.

51
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
And eating.

52
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,000
And eating eight ounces of water a day.

53
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,000
Along with making effort to eat more of the Mediterranean diet.

54
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Because the omegas and the olive oils and the fish and the legumes, the fruits and vegetables, if it's good for the heart, it's good for the mind.

55
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:22,000
So I have that I'm going to, every day I'm going to eat two vegetables, two fruits, one fish, and use olive oil.

56
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:23,000
So that's my goal.

57
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
So that's my every day challenge.

58
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Wow.

59
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Yeah.

60
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Yes.

61
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:38,000
And lastly, for the spirit, which is so important because our spirits are what keeps us going.

62
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:43,000
It is what we exude into the world, into the universe.

63
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:51,000
And so one thing that I'm going to do is I made a day, which is my Sundays, to call loved ones.

64
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Even though I talk to people throughout the week, I made a point to call and speak to loved ones.

65
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Because having that social connection as a person living with dementia is so important.

66
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:12,000
And I take time out of the day to not watch so much TV, but at least an hour of just listening to music.

67
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,000
So I'm using music therapy also.

68
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:17,000
And that's a goal of mine.

69
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
One hour a day at least of listening to music.

70
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:24,000
To get those other little sparks in my brain going.

71
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,000
And to calm me down whenever I'm feeling anxious.

72
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:35,000
And last but not least, if it's good for the spirit, it's good for the community, volunteerism.

73
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Because we can still do things.

74
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:44,000
We can still volunteer and be active members of the community.

75
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Whether it be at a food bank, whether you're volunteering at a library or at your church.

76
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:56,000
These are all good things that we can still do as people living with dementia.

77
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:01,000
And that's what I have for my New Year's resolutions.

78
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:04,000
Wow, Nia, I'm really impressed.

79
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:11,000
And something I noticed, kind of a key thing in that, was how specific you were.

80
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:17,000
And I know people talked about setting goals and the idea of making it specific.

81
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,000
So it's, you know, I'm going to listen to music.

82
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:21,000
One hour a day.

83
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
I'm going to do a classroom puzzle.

84
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:24,000
One a week.

85
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:30,000
I'm going to have two fruits and two vegetables and one fish and cook with all of that.

86
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
So you, I like how you've made it so concrete.

87
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:39,000
So it makes it easier to know whether you've actually done it or, well, I got close.

88
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,000
Or not today.

89
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:42,000
Yes.

90
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,000
They're called SMART goals.

91
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:46,000
Okay.

92
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
Specific, measurable.

93
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:49,000
Attainable.

94
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:50,000
Achievement.

95
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:51,000
Believable.

96
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:52,000
Realistic.

97
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:58,000
And time, you know, time achieved or something like that.

98
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,000
So, yeah, they're SMART goals.

99
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:05,000
I learned that many years ago that you have to be deliberate.

100
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:12,000
You know, because if you're aiming for nothing, you're not going to hit anything, you know?

101
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:15,000
Yeah, because you're not trying to go anywhere specific.

102
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
So it's just kind of aimless.

103
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
So you have set out, I'm trying to go here.

104
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:23,000
And I'm going to do it by these different steps.

105
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,000
And I'm going to do it this often.

106
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,000
And this is why.

107
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,000
Yes.

108
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
Well, I'm really excited for you.

109
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,000
Thank you.

110
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:41,000
I, you know, there's so many things that come with being a person living with dementia.

111
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:48,000
And many times people focus on the end result, which is, you know, the last stages.

112
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,000
Of dementia.

113
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:54,000
But prior to that, we can still live fruitful lives.

114
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:55,000
We still can.

115
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,000
But we have to take hold of our quality of life as well.

116
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
And not just succumb to the disease.

117
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:10,000
Well, I've heard you say, isn't it something about you're fighting to do the best you can so that you're doing, you're not just giving up.

118
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:13,000
You're kind of giving dementia a run for its money.

119
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,000
And you're making it as hard as you can for it to take things just unbearably.

120
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:18,000
You're making it work for it.

121
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,000
You're exercising.

122
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
You're paying attention to what you're eating.

123
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
You're doing things that are good for your brain and your body.

124
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
You're being and interacting with people.

125
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
You're making a difference in people's lives.

126
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
And you're right.

127
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,000
A lot of people seem to miss that middle part.

128
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
Everyone focuses on the end.

129
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,000
And some, I'm not sure what happens to the middle part.

130
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:46,000
But it looks like you're really taking that middle part and saying, and I'm going to do the best I can with what I have along the way.

131
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,000
And I admire you for that.

132
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
Thank you, Rachel.

133
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,000
I couldn't do it without people like you.

134
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:58,000
It's support and it's community and it's people working together, right?

135
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Yes.

136
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:05,000
Well, thank you for sharing your goals with us and how you have taken that time.

137
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:14,000
I know you've actually taken time and pulled back to really think and look over the past year and look at where you've gone.

138
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Thank you.

139
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:20,000
And look at where you were and where you wanted to be and how you're going to get there.

140
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
And thank you for sharing that with us.

141
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,000
Thank you, Rachel.

142
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
I appreciate you having me.

143
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:27,000
Thank you.

